# Laravel Development Rules

## Code Organization

### Controllers
- Keep controllers thin - delegate business logic to services
- Use single responsibility principle
- Return Inertia responses for frontend integration
- Use form request classes for validation

```php
// Good: Thin controller with service delegation
public function store(CreateCourseRequest $request)
{
    $course = $this->courseService->createCourse($request->validated());
    return redirect()->route('courses.show', $course);
}

// Bad: Business logic in controller
public function store(Request $request)
{
    // Complex business logic here...
}
```

### Services
- Place all business logic in service classes under `app/Services/`
- Use dependency injection for service dependencies
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Use database transactions for data integrity

```php
// Good: Service with clear responsibilities
class CourseService
{
    public function __construct(
        private CourseEnrollmentService $enrollmentService,
        private PaymentService $paymentService
    ) {}

    public function createCourse(array $data): Course
    {
        return DB::transaction(function () use ($data) {
            // Course creation logic
        }, 5);
    }
}
```

### Models
- Use Eloquent relationships properly
- Add accessors and mutators when needed
- Use scopes for common queries
- Follow naming conventions

```php
// Good: Model with relationships and scopes
class Course extends Model
{
    public function instructor()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(Instructor::class);
    }

    public function scopePublished($query)
    {
        return $query->where('status', CourseStatusType::PUBLISHED);
    }
}
```

## Request Validation

### Form Requests
- Create dedicated form request classes for complex validation
- Use validation rules consistently
- Add custom error messages when needed

```php
// Good: Dedicated form request
class CreateCourseRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function rules()
    {
        return [
            'title' => 'required|string|max:255',
            'description' => 'required|string',
            'price' => 'required|numeric|min:0',
        ];
    }
}
```

## Database Patterns

### Migrations
- Use descriptive migration names
- Add proper indexes for performance
- Use foreign key constraints
- Include rollback logic

```php
// Good: Comprehensive migration
Schema::create('courses', function (Blueprint $table) {
    $table->id();
    $table->string('title');
    $table->text('description');
    $table->decimal('price', 10, 2);
    $table->foreignId('instructor_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');
    $table->timestamps();
    
    $table->index(['instructor_id', 'status']);
});
```

### Seeders
- Use factories for test data generation
- Create realistic seed data
- Use database transactions in seeders

## Authentication & Authorization

### Multi-Role System
- Use Laravel's built-in authorization
- Create policies for complex authorization logic
- Use middleware for route protection

```php
// Good: Policy-based authorization
class CoursePolicy
{
    public function update(User $user, Course $course)
    {
        return $user->id === $course->instructor_id || $user->isAdmin();
    }
}
```

## API Development

### Inertia.js Integration
- Use Inertia responses for SPA-like experience
- Share data globally when needed
- Use proper HTTP status codes

```php
// Good: Inertia response with shared data
return Inertia::render('Courses/Index', [
    'courses' => $courses,
    'filters' => $filters,
]);
```

## Error Handling

### Exception Handling
- Use try-catch blocks for expected errors
- Log errors appropriately
- Return user-friendly error messages

```php
// Good: Proper error handling
try {
    $course = $this->courseService->createCourse($data);
} catch (ValidationException $e) {
    return back()->withErrors($e->errors());
} catch (Exception $e) {
    Log::error('Course creation failed', ['error' => $e->getMessage()]);
    return back()->with('error', 'Failed to create course');
}
```

## Performance Optimization

### Database Queries
- Use eager loading to prevent N+1 queries
- Add database indexes for frequently queried columns
- Use pagination for large datasets

```php
// Good: Eager loading
$courses = Course::with(['instructor', 'sections.lessons'])
    ->published()
    ->paginate(10);
```

### Caching
- Cache expensive operations
- Use appropriate cache tags
- Implement cache invalidation strategies

## Security Best Practices

### Input Validation
- Always validate user input
- Use CSRF protection
- Sanitize data before storage

### File Uploads
- Validate file types and sizes
- Store files securely
- Use proper file naming conventions

## Testing

### Feature Tests
- Test complete user workflows
- Use database transactions for test isolation
- Mock external services

```php
// Good: Comprehensive feature test
public function test_user_can_create_course()
{
    $user = User::factory()->instructor()->create();
    
    $this->actingAs($user)
        ->post('/courses', [
            'title' => 'Test Course',
            'description' => 'Test Description',
        ])
        ->assertRedirect()
        ->assertSessionHas('success');
        
    $this->assertDatabaseHas('courses', [
        'title' => 'Test Course',
        'instructor_id' => $user->id,
    ]);
}
```

